WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.940 --> 00:00:04.850
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:08.270
Hi.

00:00:08.270 --> 00:00:09.530
My name is Richard Vogg.

00:00:09.530 --> 00:00:11.840
And I'm happy to be
here and talk about how

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:14.060
I became a Data Composer.

00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:18.140
This ability of composing data
or creating data from scratch,

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:21.140
has been increasingly helpful
for me at my last job.

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:22.910
I was working for
a data analytics

00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:25.490
company in Chile for the
last years and the client

00:00:25.490 --> 00:00:27.410
that we were supporting
with analytics

00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:30.980
was one of the largest
banks in the United States.

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:35.270
Similar to a music composer,
who assembles musical notes

00:00:35.270 --> 00:00:37.610
and chords into
melodies and songs,

00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:40.430
I started to play
around with data sets.

00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:42.920
And using R, it's very
easy to get started.

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:45.110
It is fun to get
started as many of you

00:00:45.110 --> 00:00:47.480
might also have experienced.

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:50.420
So for example it is very
easy to create a 1,000 random

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:54.740
normally distributed variables
and we can also easily create

00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:56.570
variables from
other distributions

00:00:56.570 --> 00:00:58.610
like the exponential
distribution

00:00:58.610 --> 00:01:00.710
or we can create
categorical variables

00:01:00.710 --> 00:01:03.800
by using the sample function.

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:06.410
We can combine all those
variables in a data frame

00:01:06.410 --> 00:01:08.840
and then we have our
first, in this case,

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:12.050
nonsense data set ready.

00:01:12.050 --> 00:01:14.330
And then we can go and
take a look at the data

00:01:14.330 --> 00:01:16.760
and we can check
if what we compose

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:21.090
is actually what we wanted
to compose if it makes sense.

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:25.370
So I started to create small
melodies, small data melodies.

00:01:25.370 --> 00:01:27.740
In the beginning because it
was fun and later because

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:29.480
it was useful.

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:32.240
I used the skill in
the business world

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:35.360
to improve the communication
with non-technical business

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:36.090
partners.

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:40.250
So I created small and easy
data sets and visualizations

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:43.520
about topics that the business
partners felt comfortable with.

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:45.740
And used them to
explain concepts,

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:49.070
explain methods and in general
to create a common ground

00:01:49.070 --> 00:01:52.680
for discussion
and for questions.

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:54.990
When you compose a
song for your band,

00:01:54.990 --> 00:01:56.940
it is not enough to
have a beautiful melody

00:01:56.940 --> 00:01:59.340
for each one of the
instruments but you also

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:01.930
want them to sound
good together.

00:02:01.930 --> 00:02:05.280
So in data words, we do not
only want independent variables

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:08.910
but we also want them to make
some sort of sense together.

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:11.640
And I trained that
skill and used it a lot

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:14.280
because we were working
with sensitive data.

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:17.580
So you could not just
send data to someone else

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:18.730
with a question.

00:02:18.730 --> 00:02:21.300
But because not everyone
had the same level of access

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:22.950
to the data.

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:26.610
And instead I quickly
assembled a suitable data

00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:30.090
set for each occasion and
was able to ask questions

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:34.410
but also to give advice without
having to see the real data

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:37.380
or having to touch
the real data.

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:40.140
Another application emerged
when we had more and more agile

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:41.170
projects.

00:02:41.170 --> 00:02:43.350
So we had this aim
of having a working

00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:45.780
deliverable in each iteration.

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:47.730
And this was sometimes
hard to reach

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:50.460
because we had to wait a
lot of time for the data.

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:52.830
So we had to talk to the
data owner, sometimes

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:55.280
request accesses, we
had to bring the data

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:57.120
into the right format.

00:02:57.120 --> 00:02:59.640
And by using a
composed data set that

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:02.820
looked like the expected final
data set, we could already

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:06.420
start to work on the
output, on the report

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:10.140
and while we were still
waiting for the real data.

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:12.240
And in the end we
simply replaced

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:14.310
that composed data
set with the real data

00:03:14.310 --> 00:03:18.210
set and improved the
whole process a lot.

00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:19.770
When you want to
write a symphony,

00:03:19.770 --> 00:03:21.810
you're not just aligning
a few instruments

00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:25.080
but you have actually blocks
or groups of instruments that

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:27.510
have to sound good together.

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:29.700
And in data works that
could be a database,

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:32.250
where you have several
tables that are all

00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:35.020
connected with each other.

00:03:35.020 --> 00:03:38.100
And it is not easy to create a
complete database from scratch

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:41.610
but we still did it to
improve our internal courses.

00:03:41.610 --> 00:03:44.070
So we wanted to make
those courses closer

00:03:44.070 --> 00:03:46.620
to what we actually work with.

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:50.550
And we created a client
table, transaction table,

00:03:50.550 --> 00:03:53.670
account tables and
combined all of them.

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:56.250
And made this whole database
available to everyone

00:03:56.250 --> 00:03:57.930
in the company to
the internal courses

00:03:57.930 --> 00:04:03.370
and also for small
prototypes and projects.

00:04:03.370 --> 00:04:06.700
All this was about why data
composition is important.

00:04:06.700 --> 00:04:08.680
Now how to compose with R?

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:10.540
There are of course
the distributions,

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:12.340
I already showed
in the beginning.

00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:15.880
How easy it is to work
with a distribution

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:17.470
and there are also
a lot of functions

00:04:17.470 --> 00:04:21.190
that can help you to correlate
your simulated data to create

00:04:21.190 --> 00:04:23.440
those connections
between the variables.

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:25.300
There are functions
that help you to create

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:27.730
the relations between tables.

00:04:27.730 --> 00:04:30.490
And of course, there are a
lot of packages out there,

00:04:30.490 --> 00:04:33.130
that just wait to be
explored that can support you

00:04:33.130 --> 00:04:37.160
on your data
composition process.

00:04:37.160 --> 00:04:39.040
And as I just have
a few seconds left,

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:43.570
I wrote a blog post for each
one of those four points.

00:04:43.570 --> 00:04:47.560
I invite you to visit my
blog and there are a lot more

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:49.420
details about those topics.

00:04:49.420 --> 00:04:51.940
And I hope they might
be helpful for you

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:53.950
to become a data composer too.

00:04:53.950 --> 00:04:55.660
Thank you very much
for your attention.

00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:03.810
[MUSIC PLAYING]

